WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.200
Welcome everyone, my name is Lasse Peters and in today's lecture we're going to discuss

00:06.200 --> 00:09.680
numerical methods for model predictive control.

00:09.680 --> 00:13.680
And so this lecture assumes that you already have a good understanding of the high-level

00:13.680 --> 00:18.760
idea of model predictive control, or MPC for short, and in today's lecture we're going

00:18.760 --> 00:23.680
to look into some of the mathematical tools that are used in the background to solve these

00:23.680 --> 00:27.260
problems.

00:27.260 --> 00:31.680
And so let's begin by briefly reviewing the high-level idea of MPC.

00:31.680 --> 00:39.520
So let's look at this example of a car following a racetrack, the circular racetrack, which

00:39.520 --> 00:43.240
is given by this dashed reference.

00:43.240 --> 00:49.660
And the idea of MPC is that at every time step we look at our current state, which in

00:49.660 --> 00:56.460
this case may for example be the position and heading of this car and its velocity,

00:56.460 --> 01:03.060
and then we make a plan for a fixed or a finite time into the future.

01:03.060 --> 01:09.060
So maybe 20 or 30 time steps into the future using a model to make these predictions and

01:09.060 --> 01:13.780
optimizing our plan, our receding horizon plan.

01:13.780 --> 01:22.660
So the plan that is stretching a fixed distance into the future for a given objective.

01:22.660 --> 01:25.260
And so the high-level idea is as follows.

01:25.260 --> 01:30.720
We always take the current state and then we have some objective, for example, minimize

01:30.720 --> 01:36.260
the deviation from this reference while also maximizing progress along the track.

01:36.260 --> 01:41.600
And then this state and the objective are handed over to this MPC module that is making

01:41.600 --> 01:49.780
predictions into the future and optimizes the controls, so the inputs to the system,

01:49.780 --> 01:55.800
for example the steering and brake pedal commands, to minimize the cost, so to fulfill

01:55.800 --> 01:58.300
this objective.

01:58.300 --> 02:04.360
And then we have a plan for say a couple of time steps into the future, maybe 20 or 50,

02:04.360 --> 02:10.820
but we will only apply the very first of those inputs.

02:10.820 --> 02:12.220
And then from there we start again.

02:12.220 --> 02:19.580
So we again take the state, we plug it into our MPC module to update, to get a new receding

02:19.580 --> 02:23.680
horizon plan, so a new plan into the future.

02:23.680 --> 02:31.380
We again take only the first step, so we again only apply the first input, and so we do this

02:31.380 --> 02:33.600
in a loop.

02:33.600 --> 02:39.740
And in this framework there's one important part which we will focus on today, which is

02:39.740 --> 02:40.740
the optimizer.

02:40.740 --> 02:51.820
So the tool that is optimizing this trajectory to fulfill our objective, so to minimize the

02:51.820 --> 02:54.900
cost J.

02:54.900 --> 03:01.440
And so the mathematical formulation of this optimization in the inner loop was as follows.

03:01.440 --> 03:06.980
So we could cast this control problem as constraint optimization where our task was to minimize

03:06.980 --> 03:15.820
some cost function J by choosing suitable states x and inputs u, and this is a sequence

03:15.820 --> 03:20.700
of states and inputs u over the prediction horizon into the future where the prediction

03:20.700 --> 03:22.740
horizon is capital T.

03:22.740 --> 03:30.460
But we are not interested in arbitrary states or inputs, or we can't just choose them arbitrarily,

03:30.460 --> 03:35.380
but they are coupled because they need to be dynamically feasible.

03:35.380 --> 03:41.380
That is, the states that we are choosing, they must be achievable by the dynamics model

03:41.380 --> 03:43.020
that we have.

03:43.020 --> 03:48.180
And so, for example, if we have a car, we can steer and we can accelerate to the front

03:48.180 --> 03:53.100
and back, but we cannot immediately move sidewards because the tires are preventing us from doing

03:53.100 --> 03:54.100
that.

03:54.100 --> 04:00.660
And so we have dynamic feasibility as an equality constraint here, and then we may additionally

04:00.660 --> 04:07.980
have some state and input constraints, meaning that we can only choose certain controls,

04:07.980 --> 04:13.300
maybe we have a maximum acceleration or for the state a maximum velocity that we allow

04:13.300 --> 04:16.300
or a maximum deviation from the track.

04:16.300 --> 04:23.060
And finally, we have an initial constraint, initial state constraint, meaning that all

04:23.060 --> 04:28.860
our plans must start from this x in it, which we may get from our state estimation pipeline.

04:28.860 --> 04:33.540
So we know where we are now, and the objective is telling us what we want to do, so make

04:33.540 --> 04:39.300
progress along the track, and we have to make sure that all our states start from our current

04:39.300 --> 04:40.300
position.

04:40.300 --> 04:46.580
And just as a quick reminder on notation, we use this x1 to capital T and u1 to capital

04:46.580 --> 04:53.180
T to denote the tuple of all of these states and inputs.

04:54.180 --> 05:00.660
Okay, and so at our previous lecture where we've discussed the high-level idea of MPC,

05:00.660 --> 05:04.120
which you can also find on this channel, there we've just assumed that we have access to

05:04.120 --> 05:07.540
some software tool to solve this problem.

05:07.540 --> 05:13.360
So that lecture is focused on how can we synthesize a given, so how can we write a given control

05:13.360 --> 05:15.940
problem as an optimization problem.

05:15.940 --> 05:21.020
And then once we have that, we have this formulation that we can hand over to one of these libraries,

05:21.020 --> 05:27.780
for example, Kasady, which is a numerical optimization library or constraint optimization

05:27.780 --> 05:32.460
library, which has interfaces for C++, Python, and MATLAB, or there's also JuMP, which is

05:32.460 --> 05:34.720
a similar library in Julia.

05:34.720 --> 05:39.060
And all of these libraries work in a way that you have your problem description, so here's

05:39.060 --> 05:46.420
a very simplified toy problem of constraint optimization, and you can make a symbolic

05:46.420 --> 05:53.060
description in a very compact form of this problem, and then just hand it over to the

05:53.060 --> 05:55.740
optimizer and it will solve it for you.

05:55.740 --> 06:01.080
Now in today's lecture, we want to sort of get rid of this assumption that we just have

06:01.080 --> 06:05.860
some tool that it magically tells us the solution, instead we want to look behind the curtains

06:05.860 --> 06:11.400
of what is involved in order to solve these kinds of problems.

06:12.400 --> 06:21.980
So that's the focus of today, we will look at the numerical background of these tools.

06:21.980 --> 06:29.200
And before we dive into these methods, I want to quickly simplify the problem a little bit

06:29.200 --> 06:31.160
for the scope of this lecture.

06:31.160 --> 06:36.840
And so here we have a slightly reduced problem formulation where we don't have state and

06:36.840 --> 06:38.720
input constraints.

06:38.720 --> 06:43.840
So we will drop these constraints where the controls can only be in a specific set and

06:43.840 --> 06:46.520
the states can only be in a specific set.

06:46.520 --> 06:51.640
So the only constraints we are left with are the constraints for initial conditions and

06:51.640 --> 06:54.020
for dynamic feasibility.

06:54.020 --> 07:06.680
And so we end up with this smaller problem, which is to some extent similar to solve.

07:06.680 --> 07:12.920
And so another thing I want to remind you is that, and that's also something we've already

07:12.920 --> 07:18.520
introduced in the previous lecture, is that we will assume that our objective is time

07:18.520 --> 07:20.160
separable.

07:20.160 --> 07:28.120
That is, we don't just have an arbitrary cost function here, but we assume that we can write

07:28.120 --> 07:36.160
this cost as a sum of stage costs, meaning that there are these small g, so g of x and

07:36.160 --> 07:39.800
t, so of the state and input at each time step.

07:39.800 --> 07:42.860
And we can write the total cost as the sum of these stage costs.

07:42.860 --> 07:48.800
So the total cost over the entire horizon will be the cost at the first, at the second,

07:48.800 --> 07:51.000
plus the third, and so on.

07:51.000 --> 07:57.400
And so this will be an important assumption for some of the tools that we will develop

07:57.400 --> 08:00.080
throughout this lecture.

08:00.680 --> 08:07.640
Okay, and so before we directly dive into how we can solve the problem, I want to introduce

08:07.640 --> 08:14.280
one very important tool that we will make use of throughout this lecture, which is the

08:14.280 --> 08:16.880
idea of state elimination.

08:16.880 --> 08:23.240
And the idea of that is that we can reduce the problem even further by getting rid of

08:23.240 --> 08:29.180
the state decision variables and converting this problem that you've seen on the previous

08:29.180 --> 08:32.520
slide into an unconstrained optimization problem.

08:32.520 --> 08:36.220
And so I'm hoping that you may recognize that as something that you've already seen in other

08:36.220 --> 08:42.020
disciplines to make that more familiar to you.

08:42.020 --> 08:49.100
And so the idea of state elimination builds on this idea of forward simulation, which

08:49.100 --> 08:53.900
is that we have this very specific structure of the problem, that all the states are always

08:53.900 --> 08:58.660
connected to the previous state via the dynamics constraints.

08:58.660 --> 09:05.140
And so if we have the initial conditions, which we will be given by our state estimation

09:05.140 --> 09:13.380
pipeline, for example, and then we can get rid of all the future states by writing them

09:13.380 --> 09:15.420
in terms of control.

09:15.420 --> 09:20.860
So the idea is that in the end, we only want to have the control variables, so the u1 to

09:20.860 --> 09:27.640
t as decision variables in our optimization, and we don't want to have the states.

09:27.640 --> 09:32.900
And so the idea is that we can write trivially, we can write x1 as x in it, because that's

09:32.900 --> 09:35.560
just our initial constraint.

09:35.560 --> 09:39.880
And then x2, we can write as just simulating the dynamics one step forward in time.

09:39.880 --> 09:47.920
So we apply f, which was our dynamics function, which takes x one step into the future.

09:47.920 --> 09:53.200
And so we can apply f on the initial state with the first input, and we get the second

09:53.200 --> 09:54.200
state.

09:54.200 --> 09:58.400
And so we can do that recursively, so we can do it again for x3.

09:58.400 --> 10:02.300
We just apply two times f, where the inner f takes the initial state and applies the

10:02.300 --> 10:11.360
first input, and then the outer dynamics take that resulting state, which is now x2, and

10:11.360 --> 10:12.360
applies u2.

10:12.360 --> 10:17.280
And so we can do this one after another.

10:17.280 --> 10:28.360
And so we can define this function f t, where f t is the function that takes the initial

10:28.360 --> 10:35.520
state at time step t, and then the sequence of controls ranging from that small t, so

10:35.520 --> 10:41.880
the intermediate time step small t, to capital T, so until the end of the horizon, and it

10:41.880 --> 10:46.440
will give us the resulting trajectory, so the induced trajectory that we just get from

10:46.440 --> 10:51.600
applying these inputs in open loop from that state.

10:51.600 --> 10:59.880
And so f1 is, for example, this forward simulation function starting from the initial state,

10:59.880 --> 11:05.280
so from x1, and that's just the thing that we've seen on the previous slide.

11:05.280 --> 11:10.800
And so that exactly gives us the entire trajectory if we plug in the initial state and the entire

11:10.800 --> 11:13.360
sequence of controls.

11:13.360 --> 11:15.460
Now why is this useful?

11:15.460 --> 11:20.900
So we can use this to eliminate the state variables and rewrite our objectives in terms

11:20.900 --> 11:23.960
of only controls and initial conditions.

11:23.960 --> 11:29.180
So if we use this forward simulation function starting at the first time step, then we can

11:29.180 --> 11:36.820
write our original objective, which depended upon states and inputs, now as this new objective

11:36.820 --> 11:47.420
J tilde, which only depends on the controls you want to t, so the control sequence and

11:47.420 --> 11:55.200
the initial state, and we can do that by substituting the first, so here originally we had to plug

11:55.200 --> 12:01.420
in the sequence of states, but now instead we plug in the forward simulated sequence.

12:01.420 --> 12:08.140
So this forward simulation function f1 will give us x1 to t, and so that's exactly what

12:08.140 --> 12:12.980
we needed as the first argument to our original cost function.

12:12.980 --> 12:19.880
And so with this rewritten objective, the nice thing is that now we have an unconstrained

12:19.880 --> 12:30.020
optimization problem with fewer decision variables, so now we only depend upon these inputs you

12:30.020 --> 12:34.380
want to u t, and the initial conditions they are given, so they are not a decision variable,

12:34.380 --> 12:40.460
they are just fixed, and the nice thing is that not only do we have fewer decision variables,

12:40.460 --> 12:45.040
but we don't have any constraints, because all the constraints, so the initial conditions

12:45.040 --> 12:49.940
and the dynamics constraints, they are implicitly satisfied, because the forward simulation

12:49.940 --> 12:58.100
function will always produce dynamically feasible states.

12:58.100 --> 13:07.620
And so with this rewriting of the problem, we have just an unconstrained optimization

13:07.620 --> 13:13.860
problem that you may have already seen in other disciplines, and so you essentially

13:13.860 --> 13:18.500
get some cost landscape, here we just plotted it for two decision variables, so say you

13:18.500 --> 13:25.860
had only u1 and u2, and these were only scalars, then maybe the cost looks something like this,

13:25.860 --> 13:34.500
and your task is now to minimize this cost by taking a suitable choice of these control inputs.

13:34.500 --> 13:40.700
And in general, this cannot be done analytically, so we can't just say okay, this is the minimum,

13:40.700 --> 13:46.100
we can just step there, instead we have to take an iterative approach to approximating

13:46.100 --> 13:51.140
this, and I'm sure you've maybe already seen this in another context, where you may take

13:51.140 --> 14:02.580
gradient descent, so small steps in the steepest direction of descent, so in the opposite gradient

14:02.580 --> 14:09.260
direction towards a local minimum, or maybe even a Newton's method, and we'll get to these

14:09.260 --> 14:16.100
kinds of methods later throughout the talk.

14:16.100 --> 14:21.580
But before we do that, I want to quickly, we will do a bit of a deep dive into a special

14:21.580 --> 14:30.220
case, which is these unconstrained problems, or problems with convex quadratic costs, meaning

14:30.220 --> 14:38.340
that the cost has only up to squared terms of the decision variables, so it's a polynomial

14:38.340 --> 14:44.780
where the highest term is a squared term, and so we get these sort of, in multi-dimensions,

14:44.780 --> 14:56.140
we get these quadratic surfaces, or in one dimension you would just get a parabola, and

14:56.140 --> 15:01.700
so for these convex quadratic costs, the nice thing is that we can compute the minimum analytically,

15:01.700 --> 15:06.740
so here we could just say, if we had this entire cost landscape, we could just directly

15:06.740 --> 15:15.660
step to this global minimum by taking the gradient of this quadratic function and just

15:15.660 --> 15:20.500
setting it to zero, and so we're just solving for the point where the gradient of this function

15:20.500 --> 15:23.020
is zero.

15:23.020 --> 15:31.540
And this kind of special case also exists in optimal control, which is called the linear

15:31.540 --> 15:37.300
quadratic regulator, and so the linear quadratic regulator is a special case of an optimal

15:37.300 --> 15:43.860
control problem, where the dynamics are linear and the costs are quadratic, and so this is

15:43.860 --> 15:49.980
exactly the OCP that we've seen earlier, just with these additional assumptions, so we want

15:49.980 --> 15:59.220
to minimize our states and inputs to minimize this objective, where this objective now takes

15:59.220 --> 16:10.060
a very special form, so it's the sum of these squared terms, where the deviation of X from

16:10.060 --> 16:17.900
the origin is penalized with some cost matrix Q, and the deviation of the input, so any

16:17.900 --> 16:25.540
non-zero input is penalized with a cost matrix R, and our total cost is just the sum of these

16:25.540 --> 16:28.540
state costs over time.

16:28.540 --> 16:35.020
And then our dynamics, so these are the quadratic costs, and then the dynamics, which are linear,

16:35.020 --> 16:40.860
take this form, where the state at the next time step is always A times X at the previous

16:40.860 --> 16:47.780
time plus B times U, so this is just a linear equation of how the state evolves over time.

16:47.780 --> 16:51.660
And of course we still have our initial conditions, and the last important assumption that we

16:51.660 --> 16:59.740
will make use of here is that Q must be symmetric, so Q must equal its transpose, and must also

16:59.740 --> 17:06.220
be positive semidefinite, which means that it must have eigenvalues greater or equal

17:06.220 --> 17:14.740
to zero, and R must be strictly positive definite, meaning that it must have eigenvalues only

17:14.740 --> 17:17.520
greater than zero.

17:17.520 --> 17:21.800
With that problem description at hand, we can attempt to solve the LQR problem, and

17:21.800 --> 17:27.140
the first approach that I want to show here is the so-called batch solution.

17:27.140 --> 17:34.320
And so the batch solution attempts to solve for all controls at once, so in one batch

17:34.320 --> 17:42.800
for the entire horizon, and it does so by exactly exploiting this idea of state elimination

17:42.800 --> 17:47.720
that we've seen earlier, and this will allow us to cast the LQR problem essentially as

17:47.720 --> 17:51.080
a linearly squares problem.

17:51.080 --> 17:56.240
So let's look at what the forward simulation idea looks like for the linear dynamics.

17:56.240 --> 18:01.240
And so this is exactly what we've seen earlier.

18:01.240 --> 18:07.220
For X1 we just get the initial state, and then for the later states we can exploit this

18:07.220 --> 18:09.760
very special structure of the linear dynamics.

18:09.760 --> 18:16.580
So for X2 it's just A times the initial state plus B times U1.

18:16.580 --> 18:21.860
And then for all the later states we just always take the previous expression, which

18:21.860 --> 18:27.200
is the previous state, we multiply it from the left with this A matrix, and then we add

18:27.200 --> 18:31.120
B times the input at that time.

18:31.120 --> 18:37.600
And so because of this very special structure of the dynamics, we can write it down in this

18:37.600 --> 18:42.380
compact expression for an arbitrary time step into the future all the way up to capital

18:42.380 --> 18:53.180
T. And now using this forward simulation idea, we can also write the forward simulation function

18:53.180 --> 18:55.940
as a single matrix equation.

18:55.940 --> 19:03.700
So here we just stacked all of the states into one long vector, so that we call this

19:03.700 --> 19:05.400
now capital X.

19:05.400 --> 19:13.060
And then we can, so this is the trajectory that we want to find, and we can find this

19:13.060 --> 19:19.600
trajectory with this matrix equation simply by multiplying the initial state with this

19:19.600 --> 19:27.560
stacked matrix, which we call A over bar, and then we collect all the terms that were

19:27.560 --> 19:33.120
multiplied with U into a capital, so B over bar matrix.

19:33.120 --> 19:35.460
And then we also have the stacked inputs here.

19:35.460 --> 19:40.800
And so you can convince yourself that this matrix expression is exactly doing the same

19:40.800 --> 19:42.500
thing as we've seen on the previous slide.

19:42.500 --> 19:51.480
So for example, X1 is just the identity matrix times X in it, and then there's no, these

19:51.480 --> 19:53.740
entries are all zero, so no input is applied.

19:53.740 --> 19:59.940
And then X2 is just A times the initial state plus B times U1, and all the other entries

19:59.940 --> 20:06.660
are zero, so all the other U's get multiplied with zeros here.

20:06.660 --> 20:13.000
And with this matrix equation at hand, we can just write the entire forward simulation

20:13.000 --> 20:18.560
as this compact matrix expression.

20:18.560 --> 20:26.720
And for this sort of matrix form of our problem, where we've now stacked all our states and

20:26.720 --> 20:33.620
inputs into long vectors, we can also define new cost matrices, and so we will form this

20:33.620 --> 20:42.540
block diagonal Q over bar, which just consists of the Q's at every time step stacked along

20:42.540 --> 20:50.040
the diagonal as block matrices, and then R over bar just stacks the, or takes the block

20:50.040 --> 20:55.700
diagonal of the original control cost matrices R.

20:55.900 --> 21:05.440
And with this at hand, we can write a new cost J over bar, which can now be compactly

21:05.440 --> 21:12.080
expressed only in terms of these stacked states and inputs, simply by multiplying this Q from

21:12.080 --> 21:17.080
left and right with X, and R from left and right with U.

21:17.080 --> 21:23.040
And so this looks very similar, like our original state cost, a stage cost, so the cost at a

21:23.100 --> 21:28.020
single time step, but now this expresses the cost for the entire horizon, because these

21:28.020 --> 21:32.140
are also the stacked states for the entire horizon, and the stacked controls for the

21:32.140 --> 21:33.140
entire horizon.

21:33.140 --> 21:38.580
And so this will cover, or this will represent the exact same objective, so encode the same

21:38.580 --> 21:43.260
task as we originally had.

21:43.260 --> 21:47.720
And combining this rewritten cost with the idea of forward simulation that we've seen

21:47.740 --> 21:53.420
earlier, we can now write again our tilde cost.

21:53.420 --> 21:59.900
So we take our objective and then the, so this objective in terms of the stacked states,

21:59.900 --> 22:04.660
and then we also had this forward simulation function as a matrix expression, and so we

22:04.660 --> 22:12.060
can replace each of the axes in this expression with the corresponding forward simulation.

22:12.060 --> 22:17.260
And if we rearrange the terms and sort of tidy up this expression a little bit, then

22:17.280 --> 22:29.280
we get this compact J tilde, right, so that's the cost in terms of only the sequence of

22:29.280 --> 22:38.120
all the inputs with state constraints eliminated, and we can write it as this quadratic expression

22:38.120 --> 22:39.120
in U.

22:39.120 --> 22:45.140
So U is just left and right multiplied onto this H matrix, and then there's also some

22:45.160 --> 22:48.000
linear term and a constant in U.

22:48.000 --> 22:53.600
And so this H matrix is, for example, what you see down here, so it has exactly the same

22:53.600 --> 23:02.320
shape as our original block-diagonalized control matrix, so, right.

23:02.320 --> 23:09.200
And with this reduced cost, which now only depends on the inputs but on the sequence

23:09.200 --> 23:18.380
of all the inputs, we can write the batch formulation of LQR as a linearly squares problem,

23:18.380 --> 23:28.740
and so this is the unconstrained optimization problem that represents the LQR's problem

23:28.740 --> 23:30.220
as a single batch.

23:30.220 --> 23:39.660
So we need to solve over all the inputs U in one go for our J tilde.

23:39.660 --> 23:46.180
And if we look at this, this is a quadratic expression in U, so this is a polynomial just

23:46.180 --> 23:54.520
where U are vectors, and the highest term that is showing up is a squared term.

23:54.520 --> 24:06.460
And so we can check that H is, so this is convex because H is positive definite by construction

24:06.460 --> 24:12.880
because R we assume to be positive definite and Q we assume to be positive semi-definite.

24:12.880 --> 24:19.080
And so you can show that this will always be convex, meaning that it's sort of upwards

24:19.080 --> 24:25.800
curve the cost landscape, and so that allows us to solve for the minimum, the global minimum

24:25.800 --> 24:30.320
of this expression simply by setting the gradient to zero.

24:30.320 --> 24:36.880
And so we can take the gradient here of exactly this expression, set it to zero, and then

24:36.880 --> 24:39.500
solve for the input.

24:39.500 --> 24:48.620
And so that will involve sort of bringing U star, which is the optimal controls that

24:48.620 --> 24:55.500
we are looking for, to this side, and so we need to take here the inverse of H in order

24:55.500 --> 24:56.740
to do so.

24:56.740 --> 25:01.060
Okay, that concludes the section on the batch approach to LQR.

25:01.060 --> 25:07.620
And so to summarize, this approach exploited the fact that we can eliminate the state variables

25:07.620 --> 25:12.040
with linear dynamics in a very specific way to write this problem as an unconstrained

25:12.040 --> 25:17.020
optimization problem, which we can recognize to be a linearly squares problem, and then

25:17.020 --> 25:22.860
this problem allows us, or this formulation allows us to directly solve for all controls

25:22.860 --> 25:25.060
in one batch.

25:25.060 --> 25:26.740
A few comments on this approach.

25:26.740 --> 25:32.180
